## 23. CONCLUSION

Dempster–Shafer Theory is a mathematical framework for representing and combining uncertain or incomplete evidence.

Its important concepts include:

Θ — Frame of Discernment

2^Θ — Power Set

m(A) — Basic Probability Assignment

Bel(A) — Belief Function

Pl(A) — Plausibility Function

K — Conflict Coefficient

Dempster's Rule — Evidence Combination

The key relationship is:

Bel(A) ≤ Pl(A)

When interpreted through compatible probability distributions:

Bel(A) ≤ P(A) ≤ Pl(A)

DST is especially useful in AI systems that need to combine evidence from multiple sources while representing uncertainty and ignorance.